  "content": "deterministic cleanup is a critical component of maintaining a stable and efficient inference service. when serving machine learning models, resources like gpu memory, cpu cycles, and network sockets are finite and expensive. if a service fails to release these resources predictably after a request is processed, it leads to memory leaks and fragmentation. such leaks cause the service to degrade over time, eventually resulting in out of memory errors that crash the entire container. deterministic cleanup ensures that every allocated object is destroyed at a known point in the execution lifecycle, preventing the accumulation of stale data. this predictability allows for much tighter resource management and higher throughput, as the system can immediately reuse memory for the next incoming inference task. without it, the latency of the service becomes unpredictable and the risk of cascading failures increases significantly. consistent cleanup patterns are therefore essential for building production grade systems that are both scalable and resilient. ",
